ppening across  the sf campus  and collaborations between both other  academic institutions  and industry partners as well which i  think is  is really important in driving  both vaccines and potential new  therapeutics for those folks who are  infected or to prevent new infections  thank you jeff for sharing now fred this  next question  is for you tell us about the technology  offered by your company immune  biosolutions  and how it works  yeah so um as i mentioned our facilities  uh we do have a couple of interesting  platforms  but i think the um we're quite well  known  for hacking the immune system of  chickens  this is really an amazing amazing  species to work with  definitely an interesting immune system  and actually we do know  quite a lot about this immune system  actually b cells  the b stands for bursa which is an organ  only found in birds  so definitely there's a history on of  knowledge  on this immune system so what we do is  we try to  take advantage of this unique immune  system that  derives quite a lot of diversity in  terms of antibodies very quickly  uh in the case of cova 19 definitely we  were looking at this so  we we have several platforms that we're  using  uh to generate our antibody candidates  from uh target evaluation antigen design  up to biomanufacturing so different  parts of these of these platforms were  used for  to drive these programs so we have  a platform that is using  bioinformatics in civil code design to  generate 3d peptides  so to give the immune system  a synthetic peptide but with a 3d shape  we haven't used that technology for for  covet 19 because we wanted to go  faster and the crystals were not  available uh  early on so we use a very broad spectrum  approach with our with our  with our antigen so like i said we use  mainly  uh the chicken to derive these uh these  libraries  of antibodies we have two platforms to  select the best candidates  um both uh are have advantages uh  this is for sure we have a page dis