ay for  to start with we had two types of beats  as you can see in the screen one type  was recent  these were mtvs that was our negative  control  and we had these bigger beats that were  not going sent  that were conjugated with different uh  proteins from the virus  so we could actually see after a couple  of hours  if our b cells were secreting uh these  very specific antibodies so within  uh again very typical example  uh with the with this uh with this well  after one hour you see a small a small  cloud of red fluorescence and actually  that's a  our secondary antibody that is able to  recognize  the uh the presence of an antibody and  as you can see on the lower panel after  12 after 16 hours  these antibodies are binding to the uh  non-fluorescence  conjugated with with the with the  proteins of the virus  so very rapidly within a few  days we could find these what we call  pre-validated antibodies  they were sequenced all of them several  thousands of these sequences were  of these antibodies were sequenced  analyzed by our ai  algorithms and now we are testing  uh different antibodies with different  assays in vitro and vivo  and combinations as well targeting  different parts of  of the virus and and the spike protein  um  so very very interesting to see how we  could use our platform  to answer very quickly within within a  couple of weeks  uh instead of several years as we uh as  we typically do  in oncology so definitely it's giving us  uh the opportunity to to accelerate  research  and and test our platform to its limit  and was very successful so we're very  happy about our progress  thank you fred and this next question is  for all of the panelists  based on your experience with this new  health  challenge we see what are some of the  key takeaways you can share with our  audience  and patricia we'll start with you  sure so one of the biggest takeaways for  me  of someone who runs a large shared core  facility  is rethinking the nature of how we sha