ators  and partners and to find a solution so  there's a lot of positive things coming  out of this crisis  um there's a there's another good side  for me  uh from this crisis and that's very  there that's very  uh self-centered in some way  i do not have to explain what an  antibody is  uh so that's that's a great thing for me  uh i  i used to spend at least a couple of  minutes explaining  the immune system and antibodies and  what we had to do  but to be less personal and and to  a larger extent i think the life science  sector  has been shown into another  light with the public and we saw  scientists um rise to the occasion  and be really leaders in our societies  in  every country and i think that's  that's a great news um that's that's  great for it for us  um but definitely it we have to be  now very cautious very prudent  um we've seen what accelerated research  can do the good side  but also some of the the bad side  publishing very early on  uh taking every single study as  as as facts  it has some advantages but we need to  make sure the quality of the research  is uh to the the standards that we  usually have um to go forward  but definitely it's it's been it's been  an interesting time  um hopefully we'll come wiser  and and more together than we were  before  but we still have a lot of work to do  thank you fred and also thank you  patricia and jeff all of you for this  informative  discussion we will now start the live q  a  portion of the webinar and just a  reminder to our audience members  if you have any questions you'd like to  ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen so our first  question  coming in is for you patricia for  sorting of  nuclei for coded 19th study  should the process sample be treated as  bsl1 or bsl2  well thanks for the question nuclei are  typically pretty straightforward  and on an average day we treat them  as dsl1 now for samples coming through  that are  covet pos