ppening across  the sf campus  and collaborations between both other  academic institutions  and industry partners as well which i  think is  is really important in driving  both vaccines and potential new  therapeutics for those folks who are  infected or to prevent new infections  thank you jeff for sharing now fred this  next question  is for you tell us about the technology  offered by your company immune  biosolutions  and how it works  yeah so um as i mentioned our facilities  uh we do have a couple of interesting  platforms  but i think the um we're quite well  known  for hacking the immune system of  chickens  this is really an amazing amazing  species to work with  definitely an interesting immune system  and actually we do know  quite a lot about this immune system  actually b cells  the b stands for bursa which is an organ  only found in birds  so definitely there's a history on of  knowledge  on this immune system so what we do is  we try to  take advantage of this unique immune  system that  derives quite a lot of diversity in  terms of antibodies very quickly  uh in the case of cova 19 definitely we  were looking at this so  we we have several platforms that we're  using  uh to generate our antibody candidates  from uh target evaluation antigen design  up to biomanufacturing so different  parts of these of these platforms were  used for  to drive these programs so we have  a platform that is using  bioinformatics in civil code design to  generate 3d peptides  so to give the immune system  a synthetic peptide but with a 3d shape  we haven't used that technology for for  covet 19 because we wanted to go  faster and the crystals were not  available uh  early on so we use a very broad spectrum  approach with our with our  with our antigen so like i said we use  mainly  uh the chicken to derive these uh these  libraries  of antibodies we have two platforms to  select the best candidates  um both uh are have advantages uh  this is for sure we have a page display  platform  also a direct  b cell screening approach that will show  in a few slides  and both are are combined with negative  sequencing  and ai algorithms to sort of find the  best candidates  very early on and filter the one the the  sequences of antibodies that might be  problematic for  different reasons immunogenicity for  example manufacturing  uh and and so on and after that  surprisingly  uh chicken antibodies are easy to  humanize once you know  what once you know how they have a very  conserved framework  so once you have the the right amino  acids that you don't want to change  we can derive very early on these  humanized formats  so all this is combined under a roof to  generate  antibody candidates for most of time for  oncology  and and infectious disease  we have a couple programs in oncology  that are a few months away from  from ind and we have a cd program  that is uh in in animal in vivo  validation  so we had quite a lot of experience with  uh with different viruses  different difficult targets and we  definitely applied  all this knowledge to derive very  quickly  programs against sars code too  so i i can tell you tell you about two  things about this program so we  wanted to act quickly to generate the  best candidates  to to start with so we use our chicken  approach for sure  um so we we immunize the animals collect  the b  cells and use our our b cell screening  approach  we also recruited convalescent human  patients and used about the same  approach but  this time with human b cells so this is  a typical essay that we  we do on a platform so these are very  small  uh wells what i would call nanowells  about 2.6 nanoliters  so we can uh put one b cells in each of  these  hundred thousands of nanowells and  in those wells we typically use either a  specificity assay or  functionality assay in the case of the  kovit 19  we didn't have a lot of time to generate  and test these  newly designed functional assay so we  use a specificity assay for  to start with we had two types of beats  as you can see in the screen one type  was recent  these were mtvs that was our negative  control  and we had these bigger beats that were  not going sent  that were conjugated with different uh  proteins from the virus  so we could actually see after a couple  of hours  if our b cells were secreting uh these  very specific antibodies so within  uh again very typical example  uh with the with this uh with this well  after one hour you see a small a small  cloud of red fluorescence and actually  that's a  our secondary antibody that is able to  recognize  the uh the presence of an antibody and  as you can see on the lower panel after  12 after 16 hours  these antibodies are binding to the uh  non-fluorescence  conjugated with with the with the  proteins of the virus  so very rapidly within a few  days we could find these what we call  pre-validated antibodies  they were sequenced all of them several  thousands of these sequences were  of these antibodies were sequenced  analyzed by our ai  algorithms and now we are testing  uh different antibodies with different  assays in vitro and vivo  and combinations as well targeting  different parts of  of the virus and and the spike protein  um  so very very interesting to see how we  could use our platform  to answer very quickly within within a  couple of weeks  uh instead of several years as we uh as  we typically do  in oncology so definitely it's giving us  uh the opportunity to to accelerate  research  and and test our platform to its limit  and was very successful so we're very  happy about our progress  thank you fred and this next question is  for all of the panelists  based on your experience with this new  health  challenge we see what are some of the  key takeaways you can share with our  audience  and patricia we'll start with you  sure so one of the biggest takeaways for  me  of someone who runs a large shared core  facility  is rethinking the nature of how we share  um you know i'm a one large room and i  have a lot of instruments crammed into  this space so i can get  as many people in here and as much stuff  in here for everyone to use  that isn't working out so great now  because we have to be  six feet apart and so i really have to  limit what's getting used at the same  time and  you know usually i i tend to gravitate  towards putting everything that's  similar  together in one area of the lab and now  i'm regretting this decision and  actually what i'm doing right now  is working on restructuring my lab  layout so now i don't have all the same  instruments clustered together  and it will be easier to have all the  instruments used if i enter  first like low used instruments with  more popular instruments  and so that's what that's my next task  for the next few weeks is to basically  rearrange my entire lab which is not  an easy feat and then  also being forced to work remotely  has taught us how to stay connected so  even though we're  apart it seems like we're one team  working together  and you know in the past like jeff had  mentioned earlier that he's a tight-knit  group we're also a very tight-knit group  because it's only seven of us so  we're kind of on top of each other all  the time and just checking in constantly  and having to you know switch gears a  little bit  and try to use new platforms uh more  heavily like slack and google hangouts  has really been instrumental and keeping  us all  informed of what's going on so that way  we can help all the researchers  in the lab and so i think going forward  i'm going to  investigate more and also with my team  to  see what are other platforms and what  other other tools that we can use in  order to stay connected  but yeah those are those are a couple of  my takeaways  thank you patricia and jeff how about  you could you share a little bit about  your experience with disney's  new health challenge we faced  i think um for me there's probably  well there's multiple areas but i think  there's probably three key takeaways  that i  i would want to stress right now one of  those areas is really that  this response to the pandemic has been  has been very positive and it's really  kind of re-emphasized the power of  collaborations in science  and both the collaborations within the  academic institute but also between  academic institutes and industry  partners as well as  um you know even seeing the the  collaborations between  uh potential competitors in the  industry groups it's been really  powerful to see this and  i really hope that that you know going  forward  that everybody sees the benefit of this  and  um you know we can really sustain this  down the road to move science along  quicker and  and bring new therapies and prevention  strategies to um  to the masses basically um i think the  other  area that this has really made me  recognize  um is the importance of of your team and  your family and dealing with these  [Music]  these stressors in these situations like  this we really need to take care of each  other  everybody really needs to um you know  help each other  get through these challenging times and  everybody deals with these  very differently so it's important that  we all check in with our team and  and that's not just your team at work  but your your family  uh team as well just to make sure  everybody's doing well and make sure  that  um you know we're all communicating and  getting the work done in a safe manner  and  and just you know keeping people uh you  know as happy as you can under the  conditions that that we're under  um and then lastly i think uh this is  really similar to what patricia said in  the  um you know thinking about the layout of  the lab we were  really fortunate in this um in this  situation that  our current space we were able to kind  of move things around and reconfigure  quite easily in order to operate with  minimal interactions between groups but  you know thinking about going forward  we're scheduled to move into a new  research building in a few years  and that really has me thinking about  the new space that was designed there  because this the same work that we're  doing right now would not have been  possible in the new um in that new space  and so trying to you know kind of think  how would we deal with this  if this happened two or three years from  now in that new space and how would we  arrange things we can't really change  the architecture at this point for that  new building  so we're going to have to be creative in  other ways to think about how we would  handle  a similar situation i think i can keep  going on about other areas as well but i  think these are  these are some of the important areas  that kind of come to mind right now  thank you jeff and how about you fred  can you provide a  key takeaway to share with our audience  yeah i think i'm gonna add on jeff's  comment i think we've seen  uh an amazing response from the  the scientific community openness  collaboration i've seen  private private sector academia  universities  colleges anyone wanted to  contribute in any matter they could and  that was  awesome that was an amazing time i know  it was a it was a crisis but at the same  time i think we saw  what the community could do when  we all put ourselves together and then  and aim at the same  same goal so that was to me uh really  a great thing and i hope that this  spirit will stay  a little longer and i think we  we saw the potential of working together  uh and definitely for me it's one and  one key positive effect  again to jeff's point uh team  uh is really important we always say  as a company that our team is one of our  is the best asset that we have and  definitely in this  type of crisis we saw what our team  could do  um and it was tremendous we were highly  motivated  everyone understood what was the goal so  it was an amazing time for our team to  just  put the pieces together interact with  collaborators  and partners and to find a solution so  there's a lot of positive things coming  out of this crisis  um there's a there's another good side  for me  uh from this crisis and that's very  there that's very  uh self-centered in some way  i do not have to explain what an  antibody is  uh so that's that's a great thing for me  uh i  i used to spend at least a couple of  minutes explaining  the immune system and antibodies and  what we had to do  but to be less personal and and to  a larger extent i think the life science  sector  has been shown into another  light with the public and we saw  scientists um rise to the occasion  and be really leaders in our societies  in  every country and i think that's  that's a great news um that's that's  great for it for us  um but definitely it we have to be  now very cautious very prudent  um we've seen what accelerated research  can do the good side  but also some of the the bad side  publishing very early on  uh taking every single study as  as as facts  it has some advantages but we need to  make sure the quality of the research  is uh to the the standards that we  usually have um to go forward  but definitely it's it's been it's been  an interesting time  um hopefully we'll come wiser  and and more together than we were  before  but we still have a lot of work to do  thank you fred and also thank you  patricia and jeff all of you for this  informative  discussion we will now start the live q  a  portion of the webinar and just a  reminder to our audience members  if you have any questions you'd like to  ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen so our first  question  coming in is for you patricia for  sorting of  nuclei for coded 19th study  should the process sample be treated as  bsl1 or bsl2  well thanks for the question nuclei are  typically pretty straightforward  and on an average day we treat them  as dsl1 now for samples coming through  that are  covet positive infected you might want  to take some extra precautions  and sort them as a bsl2  just that abundance of caution but you  know it's more important of how you're  dissociating the samples because  to isolate the nuclei you often have to  use ultracentrifugation which  can be very hazardous if you're using  you know higher biosafety level samples  and so  wherever you're getting the samples from  you should use the appropriate biosafety  level lab to isolate the nuclei and then  once you have the isolated nuclei  i think you know for our purposes it  would be fine to sort those nuclei  and a biosafety level to um so a sorter  that's inside of a biosafety cabinet  so yeah  thank you patricia and this next  question is for you fred  are there any advantages or  disadvantages  possibly in screening covid19 antibodies  using  phase display versus using b cells  that's actually a very good question and  the the main answer is that  both approach have benefits and  shortcoming  we preferred the b cell screening  approach for  covet 19 not because the fake display we  weren't confident  in getting good antibodies from the page  display  but in terms of timing um the visa  screening was  a little shorter uh we were saving maybe  one or two weeks so that's not a lot but  in this crisis uh two weeks is a lot of  time  um but if i answer more generally  um we typically uh we sometimes do  both for for a single program so  definitely there's  there's advantages and good antibodies  usually show up in both  approaches but let's say we are  doing a program to find antagonist  antibodies against  a gpcr in oncology would  probably uh prefer a vsat screening  approach with a spec  with a functionality essay whereas uh we  have done  uh very difficult programs where we know  the immune response is  is very slight there's not a lot of of  good antibodies  and one example i have in mind is we did  an rna  antibody so basically an antibody that  could recognize sequence specifically  um a small rna and you can expect from  from that example that there's not a lot  of immune response against  nucleic acids so in that case we use  phage display because we're looking  we needed to enrich and to find these  rare binders  and that's where the the face display  platform is so powerful  so basically in both cases we would  probably have found  very good antibodies using either phase  display or b-cell screening but  we took b-sub-speeding because we're  saving two weeks  thank you fred and i want to thank our  audience for these great questions  coming in we have time for one more and  we'll turn to you jeff  based on your experience so far can you  comment on what type of flow  cytometry assays are useful to monitor  the efficacy  of an antiviral therapy against covid19  this is a great question um  so i think right now uh there is no  great biomarker that would define uh  a optimal therapy whether that's a  vaccine or  a drug that would stop the virus  and so in light of that what i'm seeing  is people sort of going down two roads  one is  looking at antigen-specific t cell  responses  um using cytokine flow cytometry to look  at cd4 and cd8 t cell responses to the  peptides  from stars cov2 and asking how many  cytokines those  anagen specific t cells produce how  broad  is that response meaning how many  different peptides  are being recognized uh and then i think  on the other side  is the immunophenotyping and this is  looking  at activation markers the frequency of  different immune cell subsets  that are present in patients who  respond better to the vaccines or  produce a broader  or larger number of antigen specific t  cells  or who suppress the virus more quickly  on an antiviral therapy  and looking at those markers of immune  activation  looking at the maturity of the t cells  and the b cells  and probably we need to spend some time  looking at the the myeloid compartment  and then k  cells too to understand what their role  is in fighting this infection  but all of this really is aimed at  high parameter flow cytometry so whether  that's  doing something on a on a scytof or um  or  traditional flow cytometry with  fluorescent markers  um pretty much everybody seems to be  going the route of high parameters so  we're  we're in the process of making a 26  color  or actually pretty far along now and  making a 26 color  um cytokine photo cytometry panel to  look at  antigen specific responses and then we  have a 35 color  panel that we're using to look at  maturation and activation  of the various different immune cell  subsets so we can look at t cells b  cells nk  cells and myeloids um in a single panel  and a lot of this is is really aimed at  screening  patients and then understanding their  disease trajectory  or their vaccine response  after one week one month three months  figure out how long these  responses remain and to what  magnitude they reach  thank you jess and i want to thank each  of you again dr  fred the duke dr jeff millish patricia  rogers for your participation today and  your contributions to the scientific  community  and i also want to thank lab roots and  our sponsor sony  biotechnology for underwriting today's  educational webcast  before we go i also want to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period  will be addressed by our speakers via  the contact information you provided at  the time of registration  and this webcast can be viewed on demand  levers will alert you via email when it  is available for replay  and we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time take care be safe bye
 

